{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>The Sidonians call Hermon Sirion</b>, and elsewhere it says (Deuteronomy 4:48) \"as far as Mount Siyon, that is, Hermon\" — so it has four names. This is to declare the praise of the land of Israel, that four kingdoms would boast of it, each one saying, \"It shall be called by my name,\" and each one saying, \"It shall be called by my name.\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 3:9), from aggadic sources (Sifrei Ekev 37). And it is possible that this praise is indeed so, but the principal intent of the verse is to say that the Sidonians — the sons of the firstborn of Canaan — would call Hermon \"Sirion\" when it was in their possession formerly, while the Amorite who dwells in it now, from whom Israel will capture it, would call it \"Senir\" — and it means \"snow\" in the Canaanite tongue, as Rashi testifies concerning it, and so Onkelos translates (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 3:9). And because it is a mountain of snow, and on account of the cold, it would be called a herem (consecrated/banned), and it is called Hermon as a derivative name. And perhaps also the term Sirion in the Sidonian tongue is a name indicating this. And the name of the mountain is Siyon. Now the verse that says (Song of Songs 4:8) \"from the peak of Senir and Hermon\" calls it by two derivative names: from the peak of the snow and the herem. Or its meaning is: from the peak of Senir and from the entire mountain. And likewise what is said in Chronicles (I Chronicles 5:23): \"And the children of half the tribe of Manasseh dwelt in the land from Bashan to Baal-Hermon and Senir and Mount Hermon — they were many\" — its interpretation too is: and Senir, and the entire mountain, which is Mount Hermon. But <b>Baal-Hermon</b> — they called a city or a tower by the name Baal, or there was worship of one of the Baalim there. And it is possible that after Israel entered the land, some of the names were changed, and that great mountain — they called the high portion at its peak Senir and the second portion Hermon. Or they are other mountains that were given these names after the Torah, and many instances are like this, as in: \"and Hermons, from Mount Mits'ar\" (Psalms 42:7)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "Ramban's etymological argument links חֶרְמוֹן to חֵרֶם (banned/consecrated), rendered here through the Hebrew root rather than any single English word, since he plays on the dual connotation of consecration and cold-denial."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:44:54.419457009Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}